349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (The project aims to modernize the HugeGraph query engine by migrating its core runtime stack from Java 11, TinkerPop 3.5.x, and Groovy 3 to a contemporary platform based on Java 17, TinkerPop 3.7 or 3.8, and Groovy 4. This effort focuses on resolving structural limitations introduced by dependency lag, improving runtime stability under newer JVM constraints, and redesigning the script execution security model. A central goal of the project is to replace the existing blacklist-based HugeGraphSecurity mechanism with a robust sandbox-oriented execution strategy that provides stronger guarantees against script-level vulnerabilities while reducing long-term maintenance complexity. In parallel, the migration will ensure that Gremlin traversal behavior and query semantics remain consistent across framework upgrades.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Although dependency upgrades and runtime adaptations have been addressed incrementally in prior discussions and issues within the HugeGraph community, a comprehensive query engine modernization has not yet been executed as a unified engineering effort. This project consolidates those scattered concerns into a systematic platform transition plan that addresses compatibility, security, and performance considerations together rather than in isolation. By aligning HugeGraph with modern JVM and TinkerPop ecosystems, the system benefits from improved garbage collection behavior, stricter runtime safety guarantees, and enhanced framework capabilities without compromising existing query workflows.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Beyond version alignment, the project emphasizes behavioral correctness and operational stability. Upgrading core query dependencies can introduce subtle semantic drift, traversal strategy changes, and runtime incompatibilities that directly affect query results. Therefore, the modernization process prioritizes compatibility validation, regression detection, and controlled refactoring of integration layers. The expected outcome is a query engine that is easier to maintain, safer to operate in multi-tenant environments, and better positioned for future evolution of both the JVM and TinkerPop frameworks.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
